# 🧰 Tools & Commands Cheat Sheet

Beginner-friendly commands with explanations. **Only run these on your own machine, files provided by the CTF, or targets the CTF explicitly authorises.**

> Tip: run `man <command>` or `<command> --help` to read the official manual for any tool.

---

## 1. Linux Basics

| Command | What it does |
|---------|--------------|
| `pwd` | Prints the folder you are currently in. |
| `ls -la` | Lists files with details (`-l`) including hidden files that start with a dot (`-a`). |
| `cd <folder>` | Moves into a folder. `cd ..` goes up one level. |
| `cat file.txt` | Prints a file's contents to the screen. |
| `less file.txt` | Opens a long file one page at a time (press `q` to quit). |
| `head -n 10 file` / `tail -n 10 file` | Shows the first / last 10 lines of a file. |
| `file mystery` | Identifies a file's real type from its contents, not its extension. |
| `strings file` | Extracts readable text from a binary file. |
| `grep "word" file` | Shows lines containing "word". Add `-i` to ignore case, `-r` to search folders. |
| `find / -name "flag*" 2>/dev/null` | Searches the system for files named `flag*`. `2>/dev/null` hides "permission denied" noise. |
| `chmod +x script.sh` | Makes a file executable. |
| `whoami` / `id` | Shows your username and group memberships. |
| `sudo -l` | Lists commands you are allowed to run with `sudo` (only on CTF machines you are authorised to use). |
| `ssh user@host -p 22` | Connects to a remote machine over SSH using the given user, host, and port. |
| `scp user@host:/path/file .` | Copies a file from a remote machine to your current folder. |

**Useful combinations**

```bash
# Count how many lines contain "error"
grep -i "error" logfile.txt | wc -l
```
- `|` (pipe) sends the output of the first command into the second.
- `wc -l` counts lines.

